Q1 — Previous Salary using LAG()
Question

Display every employee along with the salary of the previous employee, ordered by salary from lowest to highest within each department.

Query
SELECT *,
       LAG(salary) OVER(
           PARTITION BY department
           ORDER BY salary
       ) AS previous_salary
FROM employees;
Concept
LAG() → previous row
Q2 — Next Salary using LEAD()
Question

Display every employee along with the salary of the next employee, ordered by salary from lowest to highest within each department.

Query
SELECT *,
       LEAD(salary) OVER(
           PARTITION BY department
           ORDER BY salary
       ) AS next_employee_salary
FROM employees;
Concept
LEAD() → next row
Q3 — Previous Salary + Difference
Question

Display every employee along with their previous salary and the difference between their current salary and previous salary, ordered by salary from lowest to highest within each department.

Approach 1 — Write LAG() twice
SELECT *,
       LAG(salary) OVER(
           PARTITION BY department
           ORDER BY salary
       ) AS previous_salary,

       salary - LAG(salary) OVER(
           PARTITION BY department
           ORDER BY salary
       ) AS diff_salary

FROM employees;
Approach 2 — Derived Table ⭐

We practiced this because it avoids calculating LAG() twice.

SELECT *,
       salary - previous_salary AS diff_salary
FROM (
    SELECT *,
           LAG(salary) OVER(
               PARTITION BY department
               ORDER BY salary
           ) AS previous_salary
    FROM employees
) AS d1;
Important idea

The inner query creates:

previous_salary

Then the outer query can use it:

salary - previous_salary
Q4 — Next Salary + Difference
Question

Display every employee along with their next salary and the difference between their next salary and current salary, ordered by salary from lowest to highest within each department.

Query
SELECT *,
       next_salary - salary AS diff_salary
FROM (
    SELECT *,
           LEAD(salary) OVER(
               PARTITION BY department
               ORDER BY salary
           ) AS next_salary
    FROM employees
) AS d1;
Concept
LEAD() → next salary
next salary - current salary → difference
