Q8 — Percentage Change from Previous Salary
Question

Display each employee along with the percentage change in salary compared with the previous employee in their department, ordered by salary from lowest to highest.

Percentage formula
(Current Salary - Previous Salary)
---------------------------------- × 100
        Previous Salary

For example:

Previous = 50,000
Current  = 60,000

(60,000 - 50,000) / 50,000 × 100
= 20%
Query
SELECT *,
       (salary - previous_salary) / previous_salary * 100
       AS percentage_change
FROM (
    SELECT *,
           LAG(salary) OVER(
               PARTITION BY department
               ORDER BY salary
           ) AS previous_salary
    FROM employees
) AS d1;
🔥 Important LAG & LEAD Rules
LAG()
LAG(column) OVER(...)

Means:

Give me the value from the previous row.

LEAD()
LEAD(column) OVER(...)

Means:

Give me the value from the next row.

PARTITION BY
PARTITION BY department

Means each department is handled separately.

ORDER BY
ORDER BY salary

Defines what previous and next mean.

⭐ Different Approaches We Practiced
Approach 1 — Direct LAG/LEAD

Use when you only need the previous/next value:

SELECT *,
       LAG(salary) OVER(...) AS previous_salary
FROM employees;
Approach 2 — Derived Table

Use when you need to reuse the LAG/LEAD result in another calculation:

SELECT *,
       salary - previous_salary
FROM (
    SELECT *,
           LAG(salary) OVER(...) AS previous_salary
    FROM employees
) AS d1;

This is the same idea you already learned with derived subqueries:

INNER QUERY
     ↓
creates new column
     ↓
OUTER QUERY
     ↓
uses that column
Final cheat sheet
Requirement	Function
Previous row	LAG()
Next row	LEAD()
Previous salary	LAG(salary)
Next salary	LEAD(salary)
Previous within department	PARTITION BY department + LAG()
Next within department	PARTITION BY department + LEAD()
Difference using previous	LAG() + calculation
Difference using next	LEAD() + calculation
Reuse LAG/LEAD result	Derived table

